
#==============Recovery pattern for a failed member is to remove that member from the healthy cluster==============
Yes — and the earlier approach needs one correction.

If etcd3 is already registered in the healthy cluster on master-1/master-2, but instance1 lost or replaced its /var/lib/etcd data, 
etcd cannot simply start again as that same already-bootstrapped member. 
The supported recovery pattern for a failed member is to remove that member from the healthy cluster and add it back, 
then start it with fresh local data.

Since master-1 and master-2 are healthy and have quorum, do this carefully.

On master-1 or master-2, first list members:
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl \
  --endpoints=https://172.31.2.28:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/ca.pem \
  --cert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1.pem \
  --key=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1-key.pem \
  member list --write-out=table

Find the member ID corresponding to:

etcd3
https://172.31.29.155:2380

Suppose it is still:

4b6b45413087195c

Then remove only that member:

sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl \
  --endpoints=https://172.31.10.90:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/ca.pem \
  --cert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1.pem \
  --key=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1-key.pem \
  member remove de0797b09105a8c5

Then add it back:

sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl \
  --endpoints=https://172.31.10.90:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/ca.pem \
  --cert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1.pem \
  --key=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1-key.pem \
  member add etcd3 \
  --peer-urls=https://172.31.24.207:2380

The member add output is important. It should print environment variables similar to:

ETCD_NAME="etcd3"
ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER="etcd1=https://172.31.6.64:2380,etcd2=https://172.31.12.230:2380,etcd3=https://172.31.29.155:2380"
ETCD_INITIAL_ADVERTISE_PEER_URLS="https://172.31.29.155:2380"
ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER_STATE="existing"

That is exactly the two-step process etcd requires: register the member first, then start the new member against the existing cluster.

On instance1, stop etcd and clean only its local etcd data:

sudo systemctl stop etcd

sudo mv /var/lib/etcd /var/lib/etcd.old-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)

sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/etcd
sudo chown root:root /var/lib/etcd

Verify /etc/etcd.env still contains:

ETCD_NAME=etcd3
ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER_STATE=existing

ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER=etcd1=https://172.31.6.64:2380,etcd2=https://172.31.12.230:2380,etcd3=https://172.31.29.155:2380

Then start it:

sudo systemctl start etcd

Watch immediately:

sudo journalctl -fu etcd

You should no longer get:

member ... has already been bootstrapped

or:

request cluster ID mismatch

Then verify from a healthy master:

sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl \
  --endpoints=https://172.31.6.64:2379,https://172.31.12.230:2379,https://172.31.29.155:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/ca.pem \
  --cert=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1.pem \
  --key=/etc/ssl/etcd/ssl/admin-master-1-key.pem \
  endpoint health

You want:

172.31.6.64:2379      is healthy
172.31.12.230:2379    is healthy
172.31.29.155:2379    is healthy


#==============Recovery pattern for a failed member is to remove that member from the healthy cluster==============
Yes — this output is exactly what we expect immediately after re-adding etcd3.

The key row is:

| b73a59790f862e12 | unstarted |       | https://172.31.29.155:2380 | | false |

That means the healthy cluster has registered a new member ID for instance1:

b73a59790f862e12

and it is waiting for that member to start.

This is good, because the old removed member ID was:

4b6b45413087195c

So now on instance1, make sure the old local data is completely gone:

sudo systemctl stop etcd

sudo mv /var/lib/etcd /var/lib/etcd.old-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/etcd
sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd
sudo chown root:root /var/lib/etcd

Then verify:

sudo ls -la /var/lib/etcd

It should be empty.

Also verify /etc/etcd.env uses:

ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER_STATE=existing

and:

ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER=etcd1=https://172.31.6.64:2380,etcd2=https://172.31.12.230:2380,etcd3=https://172.31.29.155:2380

Then start etcd:

sudo systemctl start etcd

Watch:

sudo journalctl -fu etcd

If successful, rerun the member list from master-1. The row should change from:

b73a59790f862e12 | unstarted | ...

to something like:

b73a59790f862e12 | started | etcd3 | https://172.31.29.155:2380 | https://172.31.29.155:2379

That is the state you want before rerunning Kubespray.

So yes: the add operation succeeded. etcd3 is registered correctly now; it just has not started yet.